(* expect:
nodeId is the env value: true
nodeId is not the buildId: true
env: OEVM_NODE_ID=a1b2c3d4e5f6
*)
(* rete#186/#118: the node names its own BOARD. Sibling of 03_build_id.nsl beside it —
   buildId names the IMAGE, bootId names the RUN, this names the HARDWARE — and it
   exists because the third of those used to be a source constant. Whatever devkit
   was flashed with PortaBridgeNodeC6 wrote into the one `c6-bench` space: 36,153
   events across 19 distinct `obs/build` entries from at least two boards and two
   different apps, with nothing in the store able to say which. A porta space keyed
   on a slot cannot answer "what did THIS board do".

   On target the value is the board's factory link address read from silicon. A host
   VM has no silicon, so its stand-in is the OEVM_NODE_ID env value, set per test by
   the `env:` header above (several VMs per machine each need their own id). With the
   variable unset or empty the primitive FAILS — see 06_node_id_unset.nsl.

   The second line is the one worth having: `platform nodeId` and `platform buildId`
   are adjacent prims built by the same String idiom, and transposing the two cases
   in platform.c is a mistake that leaves both prims answering and every assertion
   about non-emptiness still passing. Comparing them catches it. *)
class NodeIdTest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | n b |
    n:: platform nodeId.
    b:: platform buildId.
    ('nodeId is the env value: ', (n = 'a1b2c3d4e5f6') printString) println.
    ('nodeId is not the buildId: ', (n = b) not printString) println.
    ^ 0 )
) : ()
